<!--
  T1110 — Brute Force (SSH)

  First attempt used a single if_matched_group="authentication_failed" correlator to aggregate across
  failure types (wrong password AND non-existent user) with one tighter threshold than the stock
  per-type correlators (5712/5719/5763, each frequency=8/120s independently). Verified via
  wazuh-logtest that if_matched_group never fires in this deployment — reproduced with a literal copy
  of the stock ruleset's own if_matched_group rule (40111, same pattern, only frequency changed) and
  it didn't fire either, while if_matched_sid (stock rule 5763) fired exactly as documented. Rather
  than chase what looks like a version-specific bug, pivoted to two explicit if_matched_sid rules
  below — same intent (tighter threshold + active response), proven mechanism.
-->
<group name="local,syslog,sshd,">

  <rule id="100010" level="12" frequency="4" timeframe="60" ignore="120">
    <if_matched_sid>5760</if_matched_sid>
    <same_source_ip />
    <description>sshd: brute force — $(srcip) had 4+ wrong-password failures in 60s.</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1110</id>
      <id>T1110.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>authentication_failures,attack,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100011" level="12" frequency="4" timeframe="60" ignore="120">
    <if_matched_sid>5710</if_matched_sid>
    <same_source_ip />
    <description>sshd: brute force — $(srcip) had 4+ non-existent-user failures in 60s.</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1110</id>
      <id>T1110.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>authentication_failures,attack,</group>
  </rule>

  <!--
  Evasion follow-up: both key on same_source_ip and a single failure type, so (a) a spray that stays
  under 4/60s from any single source, or (b) an attacker who mixes wrong-password and invalid-user
  guesses to split their attempts across 100010 and 100011 without tripping either threshold alone,
  both evade this pair by design. Documented in detection-catalog.md as a known gap, not patched here.
  -->

</group>

<!--
  T1484.001 — Group Policy Modification (SYSVOL integrity)

  Default FIM (rules 550/553/554 — modified/deleted/added) treats every monitored path identically:
  level 5-7 regardless of WHERE the change happened. That undersells SYSVOL specifically — it holds
  GPOs and logon scripts that apply to every domain-joined host on next policy refresh/logon, so a
  single unauthorized write there has a blast radius no other path in this lab has. This rule doesn't
  replace the base FIM rules (they still fire, still carry the full hash/attribute record) — it adds
  a second, higher-severity alert scoped to that one path via the `file` dynamic field syscheck
  decodes into.
-->
<group name="local,syscheck,">

  <rule id="100020" level="12">
    <if_sid>550,553,554</if_sid>
    <field name="file" type="pcre2">^/var/lib/samba/sysvol</field>
    <description>SYSVOL integrity change (GPO/logon script) — $(file)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1484.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>gpo_tampering,attack,</group>
  </rule>

  <!--
  No active response wired here on purpose — auto-reverting a file is a much riskier default action
  than blocking an IP (could clobber a legitimate GPO edit made seconds earlier), so this stays
  alert-only pending a real response playbook. See detection-catalog.md.
  -->

  <!--
  T1053.003 — Scheduled Task/Job: Cron (Persistence)

  Same escalation pattern as SYSVOL above, different path set: cron directories are where a compromised
  host most commonly gets a persistence mechanism planted (a scheduled beacon/implant re-download).
  Extended real-time FIM to cover them plus a handful of other classic Linux persistence/credential
  files (/etc/passwd, /etc/shadow, /etc/sudoers) in the same <directories> block.
  -->
  <rule id="100050" level="12">
    <if_sid>550,553,554</if_sid>
    <field name="file" type="pcre2">^/etc/cron\.(d|daily|hourly|weekly|monthly)/|^/etc/crontab$</field>
    <description>Cron persistence — $(file) added/modified/deleted.</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1053.003</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>persistence,attack,</group>
  </rule>

  <!--
  T1136.001 (Create Account: Local Account) / T1098 (Account Manipulation) — local account and
  credential-file tampering (host-level, distinct from the Samba/AD-level T1098 investigation above —
  this catches changes to the OS's OWN account files, relevant even though the primary identity plane
  here is AD, since dc-01 itself is still a Linux host with local accounts/sudoers that could be abused
  for persistence independent of AD entirely). Both MITRE IDs tagged deliberately, not sloppily: the rule
  can't distinguish creating a new local account (useradd writes both passwd+shadow) from modifying an
  existing one's password/shell (T1098) — same honest broad-not-narrow tradeoff as the rest of this FIM
  family. The verified test below specifically exercised account *creation*.
  -->
  <rule id="100051" level="12">
    <if_sid>550,553,554</if_sid>
    <field name="file" type="pcre2">^/etc/(passwd|shadow|sudoers)$</field>
    <description>Local account/credential file changed — $(file).</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1136.001</id>
      <id>T1098</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>persistence,attack,</group>
  </rule>

</group>

<!--
  T1558.003 — Kerberoasting

  Samba's default log level (0) logs nothing about individual Kerberos ticket requests. Enabled
  structured audit logging (`log level = 1 auth_audit:3 auth_json_audit:3` in smb.conf) which emits
  real per-request JSON to /var/log/samba/log.samba, including a "KDC Authorization" event per TGS-REQ
  with the requesting account and the target SPN — this is Samba's equivalent of Windows Event ID 4769.
  Wired as a `log_format=json` localfile; Wazuh's JSON parser silently skips the human-readable lines
  log.samba also writes, so no separate decoder was needed for that half.
-->
<group name="local,kerberos,">

  <rule id="100030" level="3">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="type">^KDC Authorization$</field>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <description>Samba KDC: TGS-REQ for a service ticket.</description>
    <group>kerberos_tgs,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100031" level="12" frequency="3" timeframe="60" ignore="120">
    <if_matched_sid>100030</if_matched_sid>
    <same_field>KDC Authorization.account</same_field>
    <description>Kerberoasting suspected — $(KDC Authorization.account) requested 3+ service tickets in 60s.</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1558.003</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>attack,</group>
  </rule>

  <!--
  Evasion: same_field correlation counts OCCURRENCES from one account, not DISTINCT SPNs requested --
  a real attacker who already knows exactly which one or two SPNs are worth cracking (rather than
  sweeping every SPN in the domain) stays under threshold and evades this rule entirely. That's a
  meaningful real-world case: a targeted Kerberoast against one juiciest-looking service account looks
  identical to a legitimate client requesting its one ticket. Not patched here -- see
  detection-catalog.md for why (would need baselining normal per-account SPN request patterns, out of
  scope for a rule this lab can verify against real telemetry tonight).
  -->

</group>

<!--
  T1110.001 — Brute Force: Password Guessing (Kerberos pre-authentication, distinct from the sshd-based
  100010/100011 above)

  Same ATT&CK ID, genuinely different detection surface: an attacker guessing AD account passwords via
  Kerberos (kinit, or any AD-aware tool) never touches sshd at all, so 100010/100011 are blind to this
  vector entirely. Reuses the same auth_json_audit telemetry wired up for Kerberoasting above -- Samba
  emits an eventId 4625 (matches real Windows Event ID 4625) per failed pre-auth attempt.
-->
<group name="local,kerberos,">

  <rule id="100040" level="5">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="type">^Authentication$</field>
    <field name="Authentication.eventId">^4625$</field>
    <field name="Authentication.serviceDescription">^Kerberos KDC$</field>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <description>Samba KDC: Kerberos pre-authentication failed for $(Authentication.clientAccount).</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1110.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>authentication_failed,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100041" level="12" frequency="4" timeframe="60" ignore="120">
    <if_matched_sid>100040</if_matched_sid>
    <same_field>Authentication.clientAccount</same_field>
    <description>Kerberos brute force — $(Authentication.clientAccount) had 4+ pre-auth failures in 60s.</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1110</id>
      <id>T1110.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>authentication_failures,attack,</group>
  </rule>

  <!--
  Active response: disables the targeted AD account via samba-tool rather than blocking a source IP.
  IP-blocking (used for the sshd rules above) doesn't fit here -- a Kerberos brute force can originate
  from any domain member, and blocking one source IP does nothing if the attacker just tries from
  another host. Disabling the *account* stops the attack regardless of source. Custom script since the
  stock `disable-account` active response only understands local system accounts, not Samba AD accounts.
  See disable-ad-account.sh (mirrored in this folder) and detection-catalog.md for the real risk this
  creates: an attacker who knows this rule exists can weaponize it into a DoS by deliberately failing
  Kerberos auth as a real, legitimate, high-value account (helpdesk lead, an admin) to get it locked out.
  -->

</group>
